    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "Mathematics feels both fascinating and intimidating—like a puzzle wrapped in symbols. While I enjoy structured logic and problem-solving, it often feels daunting because of its abstract nature. It’s not just about numbers; it’s about understanding systems and patterns that govern the world around us. My interest lies more in applying math to real-world problems rather than memorization.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Absolutely—I’ve had moments of frustration, especially when dealing with complex proofs or unfamiliar concepts. Anxiety spikes when I feel overwhelmed by the speed of abstraction needed in higher-level math. That said, breaking problems into smaller steps and revisiting them has helped me manage it better over time.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Not intentionally—I don’t typically rely on AI for deep learning, but if I encountered tricky algebra or calculus problems, a quick search might reveal step-by-step solutions. Though I’ve found it’s most useful for verifying my work rather than discovering new methods. Human intuition and explanation still feel more valuable.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second-order algebraic equation?": "A quadratic equation (e.g., ax² + bx + c = 0) is solved using the quadratic formula: x = [-b ± √(b² – 4ac)] / 2a. First, identify coefficients *a*, *b*, and *c*. Plug them into the formula to compute the discriminant (b² – 4ac). If positive, two real roots exist; if zero, one; if negative, complex solutions. Simplify results by rationalizing denominators.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "Stationary points occur where f'(x) = 0 or is undefined. First, differentiate *f(x)* to get f'(x). Solve f'(x) = 0 for critical points. Test intervals around these points by plugging into a second derivative test (concave up/down) or using the first derivative test (sign changes). If f'(x) doesn’t exist at a point, check there too.",
